// P03.23 review (R2): the check for a type that holds itself by value is
// near-linear when the type arguments are mixed (a type parameter next to a
// concrete type). A walk of every instance as it is written took 2^n steps
// (22 s at n = 26; Go is as slow); a finished walk is now kept by the
// instance and its resolved type arguments, so `G[P, int]` seen in one type
// and `G[Q, int]` seen in another are one walk. Each case below is 2000
// generic types deep (the n = 26 exponential case is 26 deep), under
// `timeout 3`, and reports nothing; each ends with a copy, a Clone() and a
// zero value of an instance:
//  - mixed: G_i[P, Q] { a G_{i+1}[P, int]; b G_{i+1}[int, Q] };
//  - swap: both arguments swapped on one branch;
//  - named: a plain struct instead of int;
//  - wrap: the argument grows with the depth, G_{i+1}[[1]P];
//  - nested: the argument grows through a generic struct (polynomial, so 200);
//  - bad: 1000 copies of the program of go.dev/issue/65711: 1000 errors, and
//    no walk is cached as valid that was not.
// RUN: %python %S/Inputs/gen-valid-stress.py mixed 2000 > %t.mixed.gopp
// RUN: timeout 3 %gopp check -check-j=1 --check-error-limit=0 %t.mixed.gopp
// RUN: %python %S/Inputs/gen-valid-stress.py swap 2000 > %t.swap.gopp
// RUN: timeout 3 %gopp check -check-j=1 --check-error-limit=0 %t.swap.gopp
// RUN: %python %S/Inputs/gen-valid-stress.py named 2000 > %t.named.gopp
// RUN: timeout 3 %gopp check -check-j=1 --check-error-limit=0 %t.named.gopp
// RUN: %python %S/Inputs/gen-valid-stress.py wrap 1000 > %t.wrap.gopp
// RUN: timeout 3 %gopp check -check-j=1 --check-error-limit=0 %t.wrap.gopp
// RUN: %python %S/Inputs/gen-valid-stress.py nested 200 > %t.nested.gopp
// RUN: timeout 3 %gopp check -check-j=1 --check-error-limit=0 %t.nested.gopp
// RUN: %python %S/Inputs/gen-valid-stress.py bad 1000 > %t.bad.gopp
// RUN: not timeout 3 %gopp check -check-j=1 --check-error-limit=0 %t.bad.gopp > %t.bad.out 2>&1
// RUN: grep -c "error\[E0139\]: invalid recursive type C" %t.bad.out | FileCheck --check-prefix=BAD %s
// RUN: grep -c "error\[" %t.bad.out | FileCheck --check-prefix=BAD %s
// BAD: {{^1000$}}
package main
